"""Append substantive method/cost/negative-result interpretation, not trial filler."""
import json,sys
from pathlib import Path
from docx import Document
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1];sys.path.insert(0,str(ROOT/'experiments'));import paper50 as P
p=ROOT/'paper/MEGA27-23b-50p.docx';d=Document(p);title='Outer-parity stripes and copy budgets: a finite recovery grid with local-GC failures'
assert not any(x.text==title for x in d.paragraphs)
j=json.loads((ROOT/'results/block_stripe_frontier_audit.json').read_text())
P.h1(d,title)
P.para(d,'The previous matched grid failed every long-file trial at two and three percent substitutions with three copies and one parity block. A stronger local detector cannot recover erased blocks on its own. A new local plan therefore expands two distinct redundancy allocations: copies of the entire strand and componentwise parity over interleaved stripes. The plan is frozen before outcomes, uses sixteen fresh message identities, and retains low-byte, full Fletcher and distance-five checks at the same charged 48-base block geometry. The tested grid is a finite set of software candidates, not an optimized storage frontier or an external-codec comparison. Production code and all earlier silent-wrong evidence remain unchanged.')
P.h2(d,'What an outer stripe repairs, and what it cannot repair')
P.para(d,'Divide data blocks by their index modulo the stripe count. For each stripe, append one 32-trit payload whose coordinates are the sums modulo three of its member payloads. If exactly one member is rejected and its parity block is valid, subtraction recovers that member. Two missing members in one stripe remain underdetermined. Accepted wrong payloads are not corrected by this rule. The final global Fletcher check and byte/header validation are retained, but checksum collisions remain possible. This scheme changes only the outer erasure allocation; the distance-five detector still provides its aligned-block low-order detection theorem, not a whole-file correction theorem.')
P.para(d,'We test one, four and sixteen parity stripes and one, three and five copies. Each copy first participates in deterministic base plurality, with A/C/G/T fixed tie order. A rejected block then falls back through individual copies in order. This choice is reproducible but is not an optimal likelihood decoder. Increasing copies can reduce substitution erasures while accepting a colliding candidate on fallback can still produce wrong data. More parity protects more simultaneous erasures only when they occupy separate stripes; an interleaving design does not remove clustered-noise or dropout risks.')
P.h2(d,'Matched exposure within a geometry, and the cost of changing geometry')
P.para(d,'Seed 1001457 generates eight 128-byte and eight 2048-byte messages. Each identity is used across every detector, copy budget and stripe choice. Two noise realizations per condition use strict independent substitutions, not replacement sampling that can leave a chosen base unchanged. The rates are zero, .01, .02 and .03. For each identity and stripe geometry, all detectors must pass whole-strand homopolymer at most two and GC from .45 through .55; a failed geometry would exclude all three detector versions with no replacements. No geometry fails. Every included clean file is exactly recovered before noisy interpretation. The complete grid has 3456 trials from sixteen identities, not 3456 independent source files.')
P.para(d,'Within a fixed geometry, length and random seeds pair detector masks and alternative-letter indices. Across different stripe counts, lengths differ, so masks need not be pathwise paired; matching a seed alone does not make unequal-length channel samples identical. Padding, local checks, headers, terminal payload padding, every parity block and every strand copy are charged. At 2048 bytes, five copies cost 92640, 93360 and 96240 nucleotides for one, four and sixteen stripes respectively, giving .176857, .175493 and .170241 payload bits per nucleotide. Primers, indexes, screening retries, molecules, oligo pool dropout and physical sequencing depth are absent from these costs.')
P.table(d,'2048-byte recovery at strict three-percent substitutions and five copies. Exact successes among sixteen trials (eight identities, two noise draws); all remaining trials fail loudly.',['Parity stripes','Total nt/file','Low byte','Full Fletcher','Distance five'],[['1','92640','8/16','13/16','11/16'],['4','93360','8/16','15/16','15/16'],['16','96240','9/16','16/16','16/16']])
P.h2(d,'Observed recovery gains do not make the stronger detector universally best')
P.para(d,'At the largest tested copy and parity allocation, full Fletcher and distance-five recover all sixteen long-file three-percent trials. That resolves a finite software failure in this fresh grid at a much larger budget; it does not contradict the earlier three-copy negative or guarantee a population success probability. With five copies and one stripe, full Fletcher instead recovers thirteen and distance-five eleven. Stronger low-order detection can create erasures that a limited outer code cannot repair. The extra parity changes that balance, but it does not establish superiority across noise channels, file sizes, independent source distributions or decoding algorithms.')
P.para(d,'Two new silent wrong outputs occur in low-byte 128-byte candidates with three copies at three-percent noise: message six with four stripes and message two with sixteen stripes. Exact noisy reads and wrong bytes are retained and replay-tested. Neither full Fletcher nor distance-five has a silent wrong in this grid; finite absence is not a never-wrong claim. Error severity matters alongside exact recovery and cost: a cheaper candidate with occasional undetected corruption cannot be ranked solely by its success fraction.')
P.h2(d,'Whole-strand GC is not local synthesis feasibility')
P.para(d,'The clean gate deliberately repeats the earlier whole-strand constraint rather than silently tightening it after outcomes. We additionally inspect every 48-base block and every sliding 64-base window against GC .35 through .65. Among the 144 detector/message/stripe clean geometries, thirty fail the block-local GC audit and seventy fail the sliding-window audit. Those failures are recorded, not screened out of the recovery denominator. Every whole-strand gate passes, yet many local windows are unsuitable under the stricter audit. A synthesis-feasible frontier cannot be obtained by relabeling this grid as constraint-compliant. Stratification shows that zero distance-five 2048-byte geometries pass both local gates at any tested stripe count, leaving zero jointly feasible long-file identities across the three detectors. The joint small-file counts are seven of eight at one/four stripes and six of eight at sixteen stripes. These are diagnostic constraint counts, not post-hoc filtered recovery claims.')
P.para(d,'A future screened design must charge its excluded candidates, replacement search, density loss and clean-recovery validation. The distance code operates on ternary symbols; imposing local nucleotide composition constraints may alter codeword selection or require another framing layer. Padding cannot be treated as free protection. This is a joint coding/constraint problem, not an aesthetic choice to hide difficult rows. No motif or primer compatibility gate, physical oligo length, indel model or amplification bias has been tested here.')
P.h2(d,'A descriptive nondominance set, not an optimized frontier')
P.para(d,'For each file size and rate, the result file retains candidates not strictly dominated within the tested grid on total nucleotides, exact-success count and silent-wrong count. Ties are retained. This is descriptive empirical nondominance over a small repeated-message grid. It is not statistical dominance, a confidence-bound optimum or a search over all parity allocations. Larger grids, independently held-out identities, locally feasible encoding and external validated comparators remain necessary before claiming an optimized frontier.')
P.h2(d,'Reproduction, implementation checks and limits')
P.para(d,'The audit is bounded into six chunks: run experiments/block_stripe_frontier_audit.py with --size 128 or 2048 and --stripes 1, 4 or 16, then experiments/merge_block_stripe_frontier.py. The first combined execution stopped at its runtime limit before final result publication; all six chunks were then executed to completion without changing the frozen scientific plan. Tests check clean recovery, one-erasure-per-stripe recovery and two-erasure failure, exact costs and counts, all saved silent-wrong replays and deterministic chunk merging. The plan hash, clean local-GC extrema, exclusions, all216 size/budget/rate summaries and nondominated choices are in results/block_stripe_frontier_audit.json. No historical output is replaced. This experiment adds method and result interpretation to the research body, but PDF totals still do not satisfy the separate fifty-plus content-page requirement excluding headings, appendix and references.')
P.save(d,str(p))
